@topic stdlib.std::abi::IsizeValue
title: std::abi::IsizeValue -- Type of module std::abi
summary: The Int domain -2^63..2^63-1, the values a std::abi::Isize holds: the signed address-width range, which is 64 bits on today's targets.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::abi::IsizeValue
keywords: isize range; signed address-width range; integer domain
related: stdlib.module.std::abi stdlib.std::abi::Isize concept.integer-domains

== DECLARATION
    type IsizeValue = Int in -9223372036854775808..9223372036854775807

== MODULE
stdlib/abi.bot: import std::abi, then write std::abi::IsizeValue.

== DESCRIPTION
Address/size-width domains: TARGET-DEPENDENT.

Isize is the signed, and Usize the unsigned, integer domain whose ABI width
equals the target's address size. The only supported target today is Linux
x86-64 (LINUX-X86-64-SYSCALL.md: the native backend refuses to generate the
syscall transport for any other), whose address size is 64 bits, so here and
now

  IsizeValue == I64Value's domain     -2^63 .. 2^63 - 1
  UsizeValue == U64Value's domain     0 .. 2^64 - 1

This pair of declarations is the one place that width is chosen; a target
with another address size would change exactly these two domains (and its
own architecture layer's encoders), nothing else. Each is declared directly
as an Int domain -- not as a subtype of I64Value/U64Value, and Isize/Usize
are not aliases of I64/U64 -- because the equality is a fact about the
current target, not about the types: I64 is a specifically 64-bit signed
field, Isize a signed address-width quantity.

