@topic stdlib.module.std::abi
title: std::abi -- standard namespace
summary: Fixed-width ABI integer types (std::abi::I8 ... std::abi::Usize): Ints proven in a foreign interface's range, made by checked creators such as std::abi::u32(n).
index: stdlib
symbol: std::abi
keywords: fixed width integers; int32; uint8; c integer; abi types
related: concept.integer-domains stdlib.module.std::abi::bytes stdlib.module.std::ffi::c

== DESCRIPTION
These are ABI numeric DOMAINS, not fixed-width arithmetic types. An ABI
numeric value is an ordinary mathematical Botlish Int together with the
static proof that it lies in one declared foreign-interface domain:

  x = std::abi::i32(n)     x represents exactly the Int n, and proves
                      -2^31 <= n <= 2^31 - 1
  x = std::abi::u32(n)     x represents exactly the Int n, and proves
                      0 <= n <= 2^32 - 1

Nothing else. There is no 32-bit (or 8-, 16-, 64-bit) arithmetic, no
wrapping, saturation, truncation, C promotion, implicit narrowing or
widening, undefined overflow, bitwise operation or pointer meaning anywhere
here. Each type is a nominal struct, so `std::abi::u8(255) + 1` is the
ordinary TYPE error any struct operand gets -- it is never 0 and never 256.
Computation happens in Int, and a result re-enters a domain only through a
checked (or statically proven) conversion:

  n = x.value             # back to the ordinary Int (exactly 255)
  y = std::abi::u8(n + 1)      # 256: the declared error AbiIntegerAboveRange

so fixed-width representation concerns stay at foreign boundaries:

  ordinary computation in Int
      --std::abi::u32(...)-->           std::abi::U32       checked/proven domain
      --std::abi::x86_64::from_u32-->   Register64     x86-64 transport
      --std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall-->     the kernel

The family (exact inclusive domains):

  I8     -128 .. 127
  I16    -32768 .. 32767
  I32    -2147483648 .. 2147483647
  I64    -9223372036854775808 .. 9223372036854775807
  U8     0 .. 255
  U16    0 .. 65535
  U32    0 .. 4294967295
  U64    0 .. 18446744073709551615
  Isize  signed ABI address/size-width integer
  Usize  unsigned ABI address/size-width integer

U64 is the full unsigned 64-bit domain, 2^63 .. 2^64 - 1 included: Botlish
Int is arbitrary precision, so a value above the signed 64-bit range is an
ordinary Int (a BigInt natively, when it is not a constant), never reduced
to 0 .. 2^63 - 1 for the sake of a representation.

Every type is ordinary Botlish, built from three ordinary declarations and
nothing compiler-defined:

  type I32Value = Int in -2147483648..2147483647
                              the domain (an Int subtype: range analysis,
                              completion proofs and native lowering read
                              its interval like any source domain's)
  struct I32: value: I32Value
                              the nominal type
  fn i32(value: int) -> I32 errors ...
                              the checked creator

* Creation. std::abi::i8(value) ... std::abi::usize(value) take an ordinary
  Int and either return the ABI value representing exactly that Int, or fail
  with AbiIntegerBelowRange (value < the domain's minimum) or
  AbiIntegerAboveRange (value > its maximum). Never truncation. The
  call-specific completion proof (STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md) makes a
  provably in-range argument (std::abi::u8(42)) need no handler and a
  provably out-of-range constant (std::abi::u8(256)) a compile-time
  KNOWN-ERROR; a dynamic Int keeps the ordinary obligation to handle or
  declare them. Direct construction (`std::abi::U8 {value: x}`) is equally
  sound with no privacy: a named construction must statically prove each
  field in its declared domain (STRUCTS.md), so no ABI value outside its
  domain exists.
* Back to Int. `x.value` is the mathematical Int x represents, exactly (U8
  255 -> 255, I8 -1 -> -1, U64 2^64 - 1 -> 18446744073709551615): an
  ordinary field projection, uniform over the whole family, statically typed
  with the domain (so `std::byte::from_int(x.value)` needs no handler for
  `x: std::abi::U8`). It never sign-extends, truncates or reinterprets bits.
  There is no std::abi::to_int: Botlish has no overloading, and one function
  over ten nominal types would need it (or ten *_to_int functions that only
  repeat the projection).
* Nominal. std::abi::I32 and std::abi::U32 are distinct types even where
  their values overlap; so are std::abi::I64 and std::abi::Isize, and
  std::abi::U64 and std::abi::Usize, whose domains are identical on today's
  target. `fn f(x: std::abi::Usize)` does not accept a std::abi::U64. The
  explicit route is through Int: `std::abi::usize(u.value)`, which needs no
  handler (the projection proves the domain) and has no run-time check.
* Not a calling convention. `fn f(x: std::abi::I32)` says x is
  mathematically in the signed 32-bit range; it does not fix how a Botlish
  function physically receives it (natively: the one Int field, carried in a
  register by struct scalar replacement). Machine meaning starts only at an
  explicit architecture conversion (std::abi::x86_64::from_i32, ...).
* Not C. U32 is the unsigned 32-bit domain, not "C unsigned int"; Usize
  is the target's address/size-width unsigned domain, not size_t. A later
  C layer may map its target-dependent types onto these.
* Not collection lengths. std::list::length, string lengths and indexes are
  ordinary Int; Isize/Usize are for foreign ABI boundaries only.

Memory values are not numbers and live in their own module, std::abi::bytes
(stdlib/abi/bytes.bot; ABI-BYTES.md, MUTABLE-BYTES.md): `Bytes`, an
immutable, owned, contiguous sequence of bytes, and `MutableBytes`, its
writable counterpart (a value, not a reference).

The domain types (I8Value ... UsizeValue) are members of namespace abi, like
the structs and creators: std::abi::U8Value (IMPORTS.md). A consumer either
spells one qualified after `import std::abi` (`x: std::abi::U8Value`) or
binds the short name with `import type std::abi::U8Value`; the two errors
stay program-global names, as every `error` declaration is
(hir/errordecls.tcl), hence their Abi prefix.

