@topic stdlib.module.std::abi::bytes
title: std::abi::bytes -- standard namespace
summary: Bytes and MutableBytes: owned byte sequences, immutable and writable, that foreign boundaries (syscalls, C calls) take, with their construction and snapshots.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::abi::bytes
keywords: byte array; byte buffer; binary data; raw bytes; bytes type
related: stdlib.std::str::encode_utf8 stdlib.module.std::binary stdlib.module.std::linux

== DESCRIPTION
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Bytes: the first owned ABI memory value (ABI-BYTES.md).

A Bytes is an immutable, finite sequence of bytes with an exact length,
whose contiguous ABI representation exists whenever a foreign boundary needs
it. It is an ordinary Botlish value -- copy it, alias it, store it in a
List, compare it, hash it -- with an opaque representation. It is NOT a
String (no characters, no encoding), a C string (nothing terminates it: a 0
byte is an ordinary byte and the length is explicit and authoritative), a
pointer, a borrow, a resource, a mutable buffer or an iterator.

  data = std::abi::bytes::from_list(std::str::encode_utf8("héllo\n"))   # 7 bytes
  n = std::abi::bytes::length(data)                                     # a std::abi::Usize

* Creation is EAGER. from_list(values) converts the List into storage the
  resulting Bytes owns; afterwards the List (or the String it was encoded
  from) is irrelevant -- the Bytes is not a view of it and does not retain
  it. Every element must be a std::byte::Byte and is stored exactly: there
  is no `mod 256`, no signed reinterpretation and no character coercion, so
  a List that is statically not a List of bytes is rejected by the ordinary
  typing of the parameter below.
* Equality and hashing are by the bytes: two Bytes with different contents
  are unequal. No address, buffer identity, allocation identity or sharing
  is observable, and none of the physical choices (a static constant, a heap
  buffer, one buffer shared by every alias or a copy per alias) can be told
  apart.
* The one hidden field, `storage`, is the owned byte storage (a runtime
  value of its own kind, core/bytestore.tcl, natively one allocation of
  header + length + payload). Only this module may name it: a projection or
  construction anywhere else is OPAQUE-REPRESENTATION / OPAQUE-
  CONSTRUCTION, and the machine address of the payload is available only
  through the raw architecture bridge std::abi::x86_64::from_bytes, never
  from the Bytes itself.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MutableBytes: the writable counterpart (MUTABLE-BYTES.md).

A MutableBytes is a finite sequence of bytes of one fixed length whose
CONTENTS can change, and it is a VALUE: mutable storage does not imply
reference semantics. If a program logically copies one, the copies are
independent -- changing one never changes another because the implementation
happened to hold both in the same heap object:

  a = std::abi::bytes::from_bytes(original)
  b = a                                       # a logical copy
  a2 = std::abi::bytes::replace(a, 0, x)           # a2 differs from a ...
  std::abi::bytes::freeze(b) == original           # ... and b (and a) did not change

This holds through every route a value can take -- a binding, a function
argument or result, a struct field, a List element, a generic or `any`
parameter, a closure capture -- because there is exactly one rule: a
MutableBytes' storage is never written once it is a value anyone can hold.
Every operation that changes contents (`replace` here, std::linux::read's
kernel write) works on a FRESH copy of the storage and returns that copy as
the new value. Copying a value, passing it and storing it are therefore
plain sharing (nothing needs a copy at those boundaries), and an update
costs one copy of the bytes. That eager copy is an implementation choice,
not part of the semantics: a later optimization (copy-on-write, uniqueness
or deadness analysis, return-value transfer) may skip a copy when no other
value can observe the storage, and must produce the same results.

Contrast the things that DO have identity and are meant to: a raw address
(the bits are the value) and a file descriptor or other handle (the token is
the value). (MutableArray, once a reference to a shared store, is a value.)

Equality and hashing are by the current bytes (never by storage identity). A
MutableBytes is not a Bytes, String, C string, pointer, borrow, resource,
reference, resizable vector or iterator. The length is fixed for the value's
lifetime; there is no capacity and no terminator. Nothing is ever
uninitialized: `zeroed` zero-fills.

The machine address of a payload, for a foreign call that writes it, is
available only through std::abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes (the writable
twin of from_bytes), which takes the address of the storage of the value it
is given and does NOT copy it: a raw-layer caller must hand it a storage
nothing else observes (std::linux::read does, with `detach`). That is the
raw layer's sharp edge.

Not here, deliberately: indexing, capacity, resizing, NUL-terminated strings
(CString), and any retained-pointer or lifetime story.

