== BACKENDS
native backends only (cranelift, cranelift-generic, executables); interp
and compile refuse the call at run time with NATIVE-ONLY

== NOTES
The writable twin of std::abi::x86_64::from_bytes: the same contract, but
the argument must statically be a std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes and the
kernel may write through the address. It does not copy: it hands out the
address of the storage it is given, so the caller must pass storage no other
value observes -- std::linux::read does, by detaching a private copy first
(std::abi::bytes::detach) and returning that copy as the result's data. That
is the raw layer's sharp edge; use std::linux::read or std::linux::io
instead.

@topic stdlib.module.std::abi::x86_64
title: std::abi::x86_64 -- standard namespace
summary: The x86-64 register word std::abi::x86_64::Register64: conversions from Int and ABI integers, the signed reading back, and the native-only address bridges.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::abi::x86_64
keywords: register; x86-64; syscall arguments; machine word
related: stdlib.std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall stdlib.module.std::abi stdlib.module.std::abi::aarch64

== DESCRIPTION
Register64 is one 64-bit x86-64 register's contents, and nothing else: a bit
pattern carried across an architecture boundary (today, only
std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall's register inputs and its rax result). It
is ordinary Botlish: a nominal struct whose one field holds the register's
contents under the signed (two's-complement) reading, typed with the
source-defined domain Register64Word below. That field type is what keeps
the value sound with no privileged code at all: a construction `Register64
{word: x}` anywhere must statically prove x in the domain (STRUCTS.md,
"Named struct construction": no implicit runtime cast, no guard), so no
Register64 can hold anything but exactly one of the 2^64 register bit
patterns.

It is transport, not a machine-integer programming model: Register64 is a
struct, so it has no arithmetic, ordering, bitwise or pointer operations (`r
+ 5` is the ordinary TYPE error any struct operand gets), no wrapping, no
promotion and no C integer semantics. The only ways in are the conversions
below (register64 from an Int, from_* from an ABI numeric value); the only
way out is to_int.

  std::abi::x86_64::register64(value: int)   Int -> Register64, the checked
                        conversion: exactly -2^63 <= value <= 2^63 - 1 is
                        accepted, and its register contents are that
                        value's 64-bit two's-complement bit pattern (so
                        -1 is 0xffffffffffffffff). Anything else is the
                        declared error Register64BelowRange /
                        Register64AboveRange -- never truncation. The
                        call-specific completion proof (STATIC-COMPLETION-
                        PROOFS.md) makes a provably in-range argument
                        (register64(39)) need no handler, and a provably
                        out-of-range literal a compile-time KNOWN-ERROR.
  std::abi::x86_64::to_int(register)         Register64 -> Int: the register
                        contents read as a signed 64-bit two's-complement
                        integer (0x27 -> 39, 0xffffffffffffffff -> -1), an
                        ordinary arbitrary-precision Int (statically known
                        to be a Register64Word). Representation only: no
                        errno or other meaning is read into it.

  std::abi::x86_64::from_i8(value) ... from_usize(value)
                        ABI numeric value -> Register64 (ABI-NUMERIC-
                        DOMAINS.md): total, no error, no check. Signed
                        values (std::abi::I8 I16 I32 I64 Isize) are sign-
                        extended to 64 bits, unsigned ones (std::abi::U8 U16
                        U32 U64 Usize) zero-extended -- see "Encoding ABI
                        numeric values" below.

Why the inverse lives here and not in a portable `abi` namespace: the signed
64-bit reading is a property of this architecture's register word, and
Botlish has no overloading, so a portable std::abi::to_int(Register64) would
tie `abi` to x86-64 (another architecture's register type could not be
accepted without redefining it). Each architecture's transport layer owns
its own register type and its conversions.

The x86-64-specific *semantics* (that a register is 64 bits, read signed)
are all here, in source. The only privileged operation is the kernel
transition itself, std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall (core/linuxabi.tcl, the
native backend's helper in native/src/runtime/syscall.rs), which takes and
returns Register64 values and nothing else. stdlib/abi/aarch64.bot is the
same layer for AArch64 (LINUX-SYSCALL-TRANSPORTS.md).

