== DESCRIPTION
Int -> Byte: the ordinary explicitly-fallible conversion. VALUE is declared
"value: int" (STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md item 3): a declared parameter type
demands *static* proof at every call site (STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md) --
sound here because every actual call site in this codebase now has a
statically provable Int (or Byte, itself a subtype) in hand, thanks to this
same milestone's own producer-side fixes (std::str::encode_utf8's truthful
List[Byte] result, std::char::scalar_value's own int result), rather than
weakening this conversion back to an untyped escape hatch to accommodate an
under-typed caller.

The call-specific completion proof (hir/completions.tcl) computes each exact
call site's own *effective* error set from its own argument facts: a
statically provable in-range value (std::byte::from_int(48)) needs no
handler and no enclosing "errors" declaration at all (its own declared
BelowRange/AboveRange are both proven impossible for that one call); a
statically provable out-of-range literal (std::byte::from_int(999)) is a
compile-time KNOWN-ERROR, not a call a handler can make legal. A genuinely
dynamic value keeps its full runtime error-completion obligation, produces
the matching declared error when actually out of range, and never truncates
or masks.

== NOTES
Never truncates or wraps. The completion proof works per call: a
provably in-range argument (a literal 48, a value guarded to 0..255)
needs no handler, and a provably out-of-range constant (999) is
rejected as KNOWN-ERROR; any other Int keeps both errors to handle or
admit. Its parameter is declared `value: int`, so the argument must be
proven an Int.

