@topic stdlib.module.std::darwin
title: std::darwin -- standard namespace
summary: The macOS (Darwin) platform's low-level I/O operations, thin ordinary-Botlish wrappers over Apple's public C library, libSystem (MACOS-AARCH64-MILE.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::darwin

== DESCRIPTION
  std::darwin::write(fd, data)     write(2): ONE libSystem call; a
                                   WriteResult
  std::darwin::read(fd, data)      read(2): ONE call into a logical copy of
                                   DATA; a ReadResult holding the copy
  std::darwin::open(path, access)  open(2) of an existing file; an OpenResult
  std::darwin::create(path, access, mode)
                                   open(2) with O_CREAT and O_TRUNC, MODE the
                                   new file's permissions; an OpenResult
  std::darwin::create_new(path, access, mode)
                                   the same with O_EXCL instead of O_TRUNC: it
                                   fails (EEXIST) when the file exists
  std::darwin::close(fd)           close(2); a CloseResult
  std::darwin::errno()             the calling thread's errno, as it is now

libSystem is the boundary. Darwin's kernel interface is private and changes
between releases; its stable interface is the C library, so this module
calls the C functions -- write, read, open, close and __error -- through
ordinary `extern c fn` declarations of their prototypes (C-FFI.md), linked
from the system library every macOS executable links: no syscall number, no
`svc #0x80`, no transport of its own, and no compiler knowledge of I/O. It
is the Linux module's sibling in shape (stdlib/linux.bot: one call, the raw
outcome, a logical copy for read) but not in convention:

* Darwin reports a failure as C does -- the call returns -1 and the reason
  is in errno -- where Linux's raw syscall returns -errno in the result
  register. A result here never encodes an errno: each one holds the C
  result (count, descriptor or status, -1 on failure) and, separately, the
  errno a failed call left (0 when it succeeded: errno is never read after a
  success, where its value means nothing).
* errno is per thread: the C macro is `(*__error())`, a function returning
  the address of the calling thread's int. errno() reads it through a
  native view of a one-field layout (NATIVE-LAYOUT-VIEWS.md) of that
  address: no pointer arithmetic, no dereference operator, the pointer
  never kept. A wrapper reads it right after the failed call returns,
  before it calls anything else (a later C call could change it), on the
  same thread (a Botlish program runs on one native thread: coroutines
  switch stacks, never threads) and with no possible suspension between
  (a yield is a statement of the coroutine's own body, never inside these
  functions). Nothing the compiler inserts runs between the call and the
  read either: read's write-back value is fields, its MutableBytes built
  only after errno() (C-FFI-ERROR-CAPTURE-MILESTONE-1.md), so no
  allocation, collection or runtime call can disturb errno there.

Byte storage reaches C only through the borrowing contract
(C-POINTER-BORROWING.md): write's buffer is `read noescape` (exposed as it
is, no copy), read's `write noescape` (a private copy C writes, returned as
the result's `data`; the caller's MutableBytes never changes). Address and
count come from the ONE value -- there is no count parameter -- and the
count is proven a size_t (a std::abi::Usize's value is a std::ffi::c::ULong
on macOS, LP64) with no run-time check. A path is a
std::darwin::path::DarwinPath (non-empty, no U+0000), given to open(2) as
its UTF-8 bytes and a terminating NUL, built for the call and exposed for it
alone.

open(2) is a genuine C variadic function, `int open(const char *, int,
...)`, and is declared so (VARIADICS-VERTICAL-SLICE-1.md: on Apple arm64 a
variadic call's trailing arguments travel on the stack, so a fixed
three-parameter declaration would pass the mode where open never reads it).
The creation mode is its one trailing argument, a std::ffi::c::UShort --
macOS's mode_t is unsigned 16 bits -- which C's default argument promotion
passes as an int (what open's va_arg reads). Only create and create_new pass
it, and they always do: a creation (O_CREAT) can never omit its mode, and
open passes no flag that would need one. Every descriptor is opened
close-on-exec (O_CLOEXEC).

Thin by design: ONE C call per operation, no loop, no retry (EINTR is a
failure like any other: std::io::IO's completion policy, std::darwin::io,
decides), no buffering, no descriptor ownership (closing is the caller's;
nothing is closed for it), no text. The standard streams as a context and
the portable std::io::IO implementation are std::darwin::io's.

A direct module (DIRECT-MODULES.md): its operations take any descriptor and
any path -- raw process authority -- with no context, and errno() makes a
native view (std::binary::native, designated direct). Code that calls them
is calling into a direct module and either mediates them through a context
of its own (std::darwin::io's DarwinIO does) or is declared direct itself.
The constants below are the macOS SDK's (<fcntl.h>), never Linux's:
tests/darwin-io.test compares each with the host's headers on macOS.

== SOURCE
stdlib/darwin.bot (import std::darwin). A direct module
(DIRECT-MODULES.md): it exercises raw authority.

