@topic stdlib.std::darwin::io::write_all
title: std::darwin::io::write_all -- Function of module std::darwin::io
summary: Writes every byte of BYTES to FD.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::darwin::io::write_all
related: stdlib.module.std::darwin::io

== DECLARATION
    fn write_all(fd: FileDescriptor, bytes: List[Byte], context io: DarwinIO) -> unit errors std::io::WriteFailed:

== MODULE
stdlib/darwin/io.bot: import std::darwin::io, then write
std::darwin::io::write_all or, as a method, receiver.write_all(...).

== DESCRIPTION
Writes every byte of BYTES to FD. One write(2) of the whole text in the
common case; a short write (fewer bytes than asked, but some) is completed
by writing the rest, again and again, so the caller only ever sees all or
std::io::WriteFailed. A failed write and one that makes no progress are
std::io::WriteFailed; no count or errno escapes.

It requires DarwinIO although it reads only FD: it makes the direct call
(std::darwin::write, a callable of the direct module darwin), and a direct
call is mediated by a context of this module in the function that makes it
(DIRECT-MODULES.md), never by its callers' contexts. Its callers have it.

