@topic stdlib.module.std::darwin::path
title: std::darwin::path -- standard namespace
summary: Textual macOS (Darwin) paths (CONTEXT-TRAITS.md, "Paths".
index: stdlib
symbol: std::darwin::path

== DESCRIPTION
  std::darwin::path::validate(value)     proves VALUE a DarwinPath, or
                                         std::io::path::NotAPath
  std::darwin::path::valid?(value)       true proves VALUE a DarwinPath
  std::darwin::path::concat(path, part)  PATH joined with PART: a DarwinPath
  std::darwin::path::text(path)          PATH's String form (the carrier)

DarwinPath is a refinement of str (REFINEMENT-VALUES.md): a value proven to
be a non-empty String without U+0000 -- text that may be handed to libSystem
as an ordinary C pathname once it is encoded as UTF-8 and given its
terminating NUL (std::darwin::open and std::darwin::create do that, for the
one call). It is the same String, with the String's representation, so a
validated value keeps being used as text with no conversion. It is
std::linux::path::LinuxPath's sibling, not a shared type: each platform owns
its textual path, and std::io::path::Path is what portable code names.

The proposition is textual eligibility and nothing more. It does NOT claim
that the path exists, is readable or writable, is a file or a directory,
resolves through symlinks, is canonical, is shorter than PATH_MAX or a
filesystem's name limit, is normalized the way APFS compares names, or is
meaningful relative to the current working directory: those are facts about
the environment at some moment, never about the String, and validation
observes no filesystem. Nor does it claim to represent every Darwin
directory entry: Darwin names are bytes, and arbitrary-byte paths belong to
a lower-level abstraction. Validation does not normalize: ".", "..", "./a",
"../a", "a//b" and "/a/b" are valid exactly as written.

DarwinPath satisfies the portable std::io::path::Path trait through this
module's own `concat` and `text` (TRAITS.md: the owner's declarations,
nothing else), so a function over std::io::path::Path specializes to direct
calls of them.

A DarwinPath is also a std::io::fs::FilePath
(WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-7.md): its native path is its UTF-8 bytes
(`native`), and its filesystem operations below are std::darwin::fs's on
that native path, which require std::darwin::fs::DarwinFS (so every caller
does: the requirement is derived).

