@topic stdlib.module.std::dfa
title: std::dfa -- standard namespace
summary: The protocol every declared recognizer speaks (DFA-COROUTINE-MILESTONE-2.md).
index: stdlib
symbol: std::dfa

== DESCRIPTION
  std::dfa::Symbol              the message a recognizer is resumed with
  std::dfa::UnrecognizedInput   a symbol no transition of its state takes
  std::dfa::UnexpectedEnd       the end of the input where nothing accepts

A `dfa NAME over ALPHABET:` declaration is lowered to an ordinary coroutine
function of this protocol:

  fn NAME(first: int, resume std::dfa::Symbol) -> int
          errors std::dfa::UnrecognizedInput, std::dfa::UnexpectedEnd

constructed with the token's first symbol (`coroutine {step, first} =
NAME(c)`) and resumed with one Symbol per further symbol; code -1 is the end
of the input, never a symbol. While it runs, each yield answers 0 ("taken,
send the next"). Its completion (std::coroutine::done? true) returns how
many of the symbols it was supplied are not part of its token: 0 for
`accept`, N for `accept before N`, counting actual symbols only (the end of
the input is never counted). A recognizer fails with one of the two errors
below; nothing about recognition is a value of its own. A file declaring a
recognizer imports this namespace, like any other it names.

