@topic stdlib.std::event_loop::Driver
title: std::event_loop::Driver -- Context trait of module std::event_loop
summary: The environment the loop waits in (a driver).
index: stdlib
symbol: std::event_loop::Driver
related: stdlib.module.std::event_loop

== DECLARATION
    context trait Driver:
        fn clock() -> int errors DriverFault
        fn block(deadline: int) -> int errors DriverFault
        fn take_record() -> int errors DriverFault
        fn record_gen() -> int errors DriverFault
        fn record_a() -> int errors DriverFault
        fn record_b() -> int errors DriverFault
        fn hold_record() -> int errors DriverFault
        fn release_held(slot: int) -> int errors DriverFault

== MODULE
stdlib/event_loop.bot: import std::event_loop, then write
std::event_loop::Driver.

== DESCRIPTION
The environment the loop waits in (a driver). Times are microseconds of one
monotonic clock.

* clock() reads it.
* block(DEADLINE) returns when a record is available, a wakeup arrives or
  DEADLINE passes (-1: no deadline; a deadline not after now -- 0 is always
  one -- means poll: do not wait), with the number of records available,
  or -1 when no record can ever arrive again (a script that ran out).
* take_record() makes the next available record current and returns its
  registration slot (-1: none). Records come round-robin over the
  driver's sources (one record per source in turn; one source's records in
  order); a held source yields nothing until released. record_gen,
  record_a and record_b read the current record.
* hold_record() keeps the current record, unconsumed, first in its source
  and takes nothing more from that source until release_held(SLOT) is
  called for the record's slot (Delivery::Reliable's backpressure).

A driver adds no scheduling rule: turns, ordering, revocation and ownership
are the scheduler's under every driver.

