@topic stdlib.module.std::event_loop::deterministic
title: std::event_loop::deterministic -- standard namespace
summary: A deterministic driver for std::event_loop (EVENT-LOOP-MILESTONE-1.md, "Deterministic testing").
index: stdlib
symbol: std::event_loop::deterministic

== DESCRIPTION
  with context std::event_loop::create(8)
  with context std::event_loop::deterministic::create()
  std::event_loop::deterministic::inject(10, reg, 1, 2)   a record at t=10
  std::event_loop::run()

A virtual monotonic clock and a script of native records, each released once
the clock reaches its time. block(DEADLINE) never sleeps: it advances the
clock to the next scripted record or to DEADLINE, whichever comes first.
Every visit to the driver takes one virtual microsecond, so a loop that
never sleeps still sees time pass; a task can make its segment take time
(advance). It is ordinary Botlish, with no native code, so the same
application runs under it on every backend (interp, compile,
cranelift-generic, cranelift) with identical results -- the scheduling rules
are the scheduler's, whichever driver advances it.

Its sources are registration slots: the records scripted for one slot are
delivered in script order, and take_record serves the slots that have
records round-robin, one record per slot in turn -- the same contract the
Linux driver keeps for its endpoints and watches. hold_record and
release_held model backpressure exactly (a held slot delivers nothing until
released).

Records are scripted by time; records due at the same time are released in
the order they were scripted.

