@topic stdlib.std::ffi::c::clone
title: std::ffi::c::clone -- intrinsic of namespace std::ffi::c
summary: A second alias of a C pointer (the same address, the same type), written p.clone(): the one way to have two pointers.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::ffi::c::clone
keywords: copy pointer; alias pointer; duplicate pointer
related: stdlib.module.std::ffi::c stdlib.std::ffi::c::erase concept.affine

== SIGNATURE
    std::ffi::c::clone(cptr) -> cptr

== USE
An intrinsic of namespace std::ffi::c: import std::ffi::c, then write
std::ffi::c::clone. Method syntax: receiver.clone(...) is the same call.

== BACKENDS
every backend (interp, compile, cranelift-generic, cranelift)

== NOTES
Pointers are affine, so `q = p` moves p (using p afterwards is
USE-AFTER-MOVE). clone observes p and returns another pointer to the same
address: no pointee copy, no reference count, no ownership -- C's own
rules decide what the aliases may do.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::ffi::c

p = std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Int]()
q = p.clone()
[p.null?(), q.null?()]
@value [true, true]
@end

