@topic stdlib.std::float64::round
title: std::float64::round -- intrinsic of namespace std::float64
summary: The Int nearest the Float64 X, a half to the even Int, after `import std::float64`: exact at any magnitude, and it never fails.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::float64::round
keywords: float to int; convert float; round; nearest integer; round half to even; banker's rounding
related: stdlib.module.std::float64 concept.floats stdlib.std::float64::truncate concept.decimal-rounded

== SIGNATURE
    std::float64::round(Float64) -> int

== USE
An intrinsic of namespace std::float64: import std::float64, then write
std::float64::round. Method syntax: receiver.round(...) is the same call.

== BACKENDS
every backend (interp, compile, cranelift-generic, cranelift)

== NOTES
Rounds to the nearest Int; a value exactly halfway between two Ints goes
to the even one (0.5 and -0.5 to 0, 1.5 and 2.5 to 2, -2.5 to -2) -- the
rule Float64 arithmetic and DecimalRounded already round by, so there is
one rounding rule in the language. It reads the Float64's exact binary
value, not its shortest decimal text: 0.49999999999999994 (one ulp below
0.5) rounds to 0 and 2.5000000000000004 to 3. Every Float64 is finite, so
every one rounds to some Int, held exactly however large (1.0e20 becomes
100000000000000000000); no overflow error. std::float64::truncate goes
toward zero instead (2.7 to 2, where round gives 3). There is no floor or
ceiling. Also written x.round().

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::float64

halves = [0.5, 1.5, 2.5, -2.5, 2.7, -2.7]
loop x in halves:
    [x.round(), x.truncate()]
@caption round to nearest, ties to even, beside truncate toward zero
@value [[0, 0], [2, 1], [2, 2], [-2, -2], [3, 2], [-3, -2]]
@end

