@topic stdlib.module.std::float64
title: std::float64 -- standard namespace
summary: Float64's explicit conversions (std::float64::from_int, std::float64::truncate, std::float64::round, std::float64::from_decimal_rounded) and its bounds (std::float64::min, max, abs, clamp).
index: stdlib
symbol: std::float64
keywords: float functions; float conversion; int to float; float to int; decimal to float; float min; float max; float abs; clamp; round
related: concept.floats stdlib./ concept.decimal-rounded stdlib.std::float64::round

== DESCRIPTION
The namespace's intrinsics are root natives registered under their qualified
names -- std::float64::from_int, std::float64::truncate,
std::float64::from_decimal_rounded (core/float.tcl, FLOAT64-MILESTONE-1.md)
-- and this module may never define a member of the same name
(surface/modules.tcl, DUPLICATE-NATIVE; STDLIB-NAMESPACES.md). Below are the
comparisons every program that bounds a Float64 writes, added by the Icy
Tower workload (ICY-TOWER-VERTICAL-SLICE-1.md, "Stdlib additions"):

  std::float64::min(a, b)            the smaller of A and B (A when they
                                     are equal)
  std::float64::max(a, b)            the larger of A and B (A when they
                                     are equal)
  std::float64::abs(x)               X without its sign
  std::float64::clamp(x, low, high)  X bounded to LOW..HIGH: LOW when X is
                                     below it, else HIGH when X is above
                                     that, else X

Each is a comparison and at most a negation, so none can fail: there is no
arithmetic that could leave the finite Float64s (no NonFiniteFloat), and a
Float64 has no NaN for a comparison to be unordered with. min and max are
`nomethod`: their operands are symmetric, and neither is a receiver (the
bit_and precedent, REFACTOR-WARNINGS-CLEAN.md).

== SOURCE
stdlib/float64.bot (import std::float64).

== MEMBERS
    std::float64::abs              function
    std::float64::clamp            function
    std::float64::from_decimal_rounded intrinsic -- The Float64 nearest rou...
    std::float64::from_int         intrinsic -- The Float64 nearest the Int...
    std::float64::max              function (nomethod)
    std::float64::min              function (nomethod)
    std::float64::round            intrinsic -- The Int nearest the Float64...
    std::float64::truncate         intrinsic -- The Int part of the Float64...

Each member is a topic: -help NAME (e.g. -help std::float64::abs).

== NOTES The conversions are intrinsics and the bounds (min, max, abs,
clamp) the ordinary Botlish of stdlib/float64.bot, all reached with `import
std::float64`. The conversions are the only ones between Float64 and the
other number types: Botlish never converts implicitly, so an expression
mixing an Int and a Float64 is rejected until one side is converted (and so
is one mixing a Float64 and a DecimalRounded value). The bounds never fail:
they compare and at most negate, so no NonFiniteFloat can arise. There is no
rounding mode, no Float64-to-String conversion beyond a program's printed
result, no math library beyond these, and no bit-level access to a Float64.

