@topic stdlib.std::int::from_string
title: std::int::from_string -- intrinsic of namespace std::int
summary: std::int::from_string(s): the Int whose canonical decimal text is s, else the declared error InvalidIntegerText, after `import std::int`.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::int::from_string
keywords: string to int; parse int; text to number; read int; atoi; parse number; InvalidIntegerText
related: stdlib.module.std::int stdlib.std::int::to_string rule.unhandled-errors

== SIGNATURE
    std::int::from_string(str) -> int

== ERRORS
    InvalidIntegerText

A call must handle each (on NAME:), admit it in its function's errors
clause, or be proven unable to raise it (rule.unhandled-errors).

== USE
An intrinsic of namespace std::int: import std::int, then write
std::int::from_string. Declared nomethod: method syntax does not apply
(NOMETHOD-CALL); call it as std::int::from_string(...).

== BACKENDS
every backend (interp, compile, cranelift-generic, cranelift)

== NOTES
Accepts exactly the texts std::int::to_string writes -- an optional "-"
and decimal digits, no leading zero -- so from_string(to_string(n)) is n
for every Int, of any size. Everything else completes with the builtin
declared error InvalidIntegerText, which the caller handles or declares:
the empty text, "-", "+1", "01", "-01", "-0" (zero has one text), any
space ("1 ", " 1"), a fraction or exponent ("1.0", "1e3"), "1_000", a
digit other than ASCII 0-9, and trailing text ("12x"). It never clamps,
never stops early and never reads zero for a malformed text. It is
`nomethod` (a String is not the receiver of an Int's parse): write
std::int::from_string(s), not s.from_string().

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::int

fn read(s: str) -> int:
    std::int::from_string(s):
        on InvalidIntegerText:
            -1

loop s in ["42", "-7", "98765432109876543210", "01", "+1", "1 ", "12x", ""]:
    read(s)
@value [42, -7, 98765432109876543210, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1]
@end

