@topic stdlib.module.std::io
title: std::io -- standard namespace
summary: Portable text output -- std::io::print, print_line, write_error and write_error_line -- through the std::io::IO context trait; with std::linux::io it runs on the native backends.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::io
keywords: print; output; stdout; stderr; write text; console
related: task.print-output backend.io concept.context-traits

== DESCRIPTION
  std::io::print(text)              TEXT on the environment's standard output
  std::io::print_line(text)         TEXT and a newline, as one operation
  std::io::write_error(text)        TEXT on the environment's error output
  std::io::write_error_line(text)   TEXT and a newline on the error output

Each succeeds (unit) or fails with WriteFailed; none exposes a descriptor, a
byte count, a buffer, a raw syscall result or an errno. The caller writes no
context: the operations declare `context io: IO`, the compiler derives that
every function calling them requires IO, and the program installs one
context that implements IO before the first call that needs it:

  import std::io
  import std::linux::io

  fn hello():
      std::io::print_line("Hello, world!")

  with context std::linux::io::create()
  hello()

IO is a *context trait*: a set of operations an installed context supplies,
never a value. A concrete context implements it structurally, in its own
namespace -- std::linux::io::LinuxIO through std::linux::io::write_text and
std::linux::io::write_error_text -- with no registration and no
`implements`. Where a call needs IO the compiler selects, statically, the
one installed context that implements it (two would be
AMBIGUOUS-CONTEXT-IMPLEMENTATION, none MISSING-CONTEXT), and compiles
`io.write_text(...)` below to a direct call of that context's
implementation: no dispatch, no hidden argument, no provider table at run
time.

Not here (deliberately): input, files, paths, buffering, a byte-count or
errno API. Buffering, batching and scratch storage can live behind these
operations later without any caller changing.

== SOURCE
stdlib/io.bot (import std::io).

== MEMBERS
    std::io::IO                    context trait -- The environment's text ...
    std::io::WriteFailed           error -- A portable output operation did...
    std::io::print                 function -- TEXT on the standard output.
    std::io::print_line            function -- TEXT followed by a newline o...
    std::io::write_error           function -- TEXT on the error output.
    std::io::write_error_line      function -- TEXT followed by a newline o...

Each member is a topic: -help NAME (e.g. -help std::io::IO).

== NOTES
The operations require the std::io::IO context trait, which a program
satisfies by installing a context that implements it, normally
`with context std::linux::io::create()`. That standard implementation writes
with raw Linux syscalls, so printing runs only on the native backends
(cranelift, cranelift-generic, executables); on interp and compile the
first write fails with NATIVE-ONLY. On every backend the program's final
value is still printed by main.tcl. There is no input, file or
formatting API here.

