@topic stdlib.module.std::io::fs
title: std::io::fs -- standard namespace
summary: The portable synchronous filesystem (WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-7.md).
index: stdlib
symbol: std::io::fs

== DESCRIPTION
  fn save(dir: std::io::fs::FilePath, data: std::abi::bytes::Bytes) errors std::io::path::NotAPath, std::io::fs::FileError:
      dir.concat("save.dat").write_file(data)

FilePath is an eager trait (TRAITS.md) of the textual paths whose platform
can operate on them: std::linux::path::LinuxPath and
std::windows::path::WindowsPath satisfy it through their own modules'
functions, so `save` above is specialized per witness and its `write_file`
compiles to a direct call of the witness's own (for a LinuxPath,
std::linux::path::write_file), with no wrapper, dispatch or conversion of
the path to anything but its platform's own native form.

The path's type decides its filesystem. Each witness's operations require
its own platform's filesystem context -- LinuxPath's
std::linux::fs::LinuxFS, WindowsPath's std::windows::fs::WindowsFS --
through the functions they call (CONTEXTS.md: requirements are derived), so
a program that hands `save` a LinuxPath and installs no LinuxFS is
MISSING-CONTEXT at compile time, and a WindowsPath never reaches a Linux
filesystem: compiled for Linux, std::windows is UNSUPPORTED-PLATFORM, and a
WindowsFS is the only context its operations accept. Nothing selects a
filesystem at run time, and no installed context changes which function a
call means.

Portable code receives its paths: the entry program, which installs the
platform's context, validates the text it starts from as the platform's path
(std::linux::path::validate, std::windows::path::validate) and passes it on;
portable code joins components to it (`concat`) and operates on the results.

What is here is what behaves alike on every platform, stated once:

* whole files: read_file (the bytes, of any length, read until the end of
  the file), write_file (create or replace: the file holds exactly DATA),
  create_file (DATA in a file that did not exist: AlreadyExists when the
  name exists), each opening, writing or reading to completion and
  closing, with no handle visible;
* directories: entries (every entry but "." and "..", in the order the
  operating system lists them, with its kind), create_directory,
  remove_directory (an empty one), remove_file.

Bytes are bytes: no operation applies an encoding, a newline convention or a
normalization to what it reads or writes. Names are text here: an entry
whose name has no String (Linux bytes that are not UTF-8, a Windows name
with an unpaired UTF-16 surrogate) makes `entries` fail with
std::io::path::NotText rather than be skipped or decoded lossily; the
platform modules (std::linux::fs::entries, std::windows::fs::entries) list
every name losslessly as a native path.

Not here (deliberately): handles. An opened file is an affine value of its
platform's own type (std::linux::fs::File, std::windows::fs::File), and a
trait requirement cannot return a type that differs by witness: streaming
reads and writes are the platform modules' (WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-7.md,
"Contexts and portable composition"). Nor: metadata, links, renaming,
permissions, watching, asynchronous I/O.

