LinuxPath exactly the carrier string), never an escaped or display-only
rendering; the standard formatting of a portable textual Path should equal
its `text`. Native paths -- what an operating system names a location with:
Linux's bytes, Windows' UTF-16 code units -- are a different, lower-level
abstraction, each platform's own (std::linux::fs::NativePath,
std::windows::fs::NativePath): every textual path has one, losslessly, but
not every native path has a text (NotText). std::io::fs::FilePath is the
textual paths a filesystem can operate on (WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-7.md).

