@topic stdlib.module.std::linux
title: std::linux -- standard namespace
summary: Thin Botlish wrappers over the raw Linux syscall transports: std::linux::write and std::linux::read, one syscall each with the kernel's raw result (a direct module, native only).
index: stdlib
symbol: std::linux
keywords: linux syscalls; raw io; file descriptor; write; read
related: stdlib.module.std::linux::io stdlib.module.std::linux::sys concept.direct-modules backend.native-only

== DESCRIPTION
The transports `std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall` and
`std::linux::abi::aarch64::syscall` (the privileged operations, one per
architecture) are generic kernel transitions: seven register words in, the
raw result register out, no syscall known to the compiler. This module is
policy above them, written in Botlish and the same on every architecture:
what each wrapper takes, returns and guarantees.

  std::linux::write(fd, data)    write(2): ONE syscall, the kernel's raw
                            signed result as an ordinary Int.
  std::linux::read(fd, data)     read(2): ONE syscall into the caller's
                            std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes (MUTABLE-BYTES.md); the
                            kernel's raw signed result and the updated
                            buffer come back in a std::linux::ReadResult.

What differs by architecture -- the syscall numbers (write is 1 on x86-64
and 64 on AArch64), which register carries which argument, and how a Botlish
value becomes that architecture's register contents -- is std::linux::sys,
an architecture-specific module: stdlib/linux/sys.x86_64.bot and
stdlib/linux/sys.aarch64.bot, of which the compilation target's architecture
selects the one (surface/modules.tcl's ModuleFile; -target, the host's by
default). So `std::linux::write` and everything above it (std::linux::io,
the portable std::io::print) is one source on every architecture, and
nothing here names a register or a number.

write is the first syscall that moves memory, so it is also the first that
needs the memory boundary of ABI-BYTES.md: its buffer is an
std::abi::bytes::Bytes -- an immutable, owned, contiguous byte value -- and
both the address and the byte count are derived from that ONE value. The
wrapper deliberately has no `count` parameter: a separate count could
disagree with the buffer, and `write(fd, bytes, count)` is exactly the
pointer/length mismatch the safe wrapper exists to rule out. The raw syscall
still receives the address and the count in separate registers, because that
is machine reality; the Botlish function keeps the two facts coupled.

The machine address is taken by the architecture's raw bridge
(std::abi::x86_64::from_bytes, std::abi::aarch64::from_bytes) and consumed
synchronously by the syscall in the same function of std::linux::sys: the
address is never returned, stored or retained, and the compiler keeps
`data`'s storage alive until the kernel has returned (native/lower.tcl's
`keepalive`). Nothing here can retain a pointer after the call -- that
belongs to a future resource/context design.

Syscall-level semantics, deliberately thin (no errno layer yet):

* The result is the raw signed result register as an Int: n >= 0 is the
  number of bytes written; a negative n is the kernel's -errno (-9 for a
  bad file descriptor on Linux, on both architectures: the generic errno
  table), preserved exactly -- never reread as an unsigned number, never
  turned into -1/errno. A later, higher-level wrapper can translate
  negative results into Botlish errors.
* ONE write call. A successful write may write fewer bytes than the Bytes
  holds (a pipe, a signal, a full disk): the wrapper does not loop, retry
  or complete the write; the result says how much happened. Completion is
  a policy for a higher I/O abstraction.
* Native only, on the target's architecture: the Tcl backends refuse the
  syscall (NATIVE-ONLY) exactly as they refuse getpid; nothing imitates a
  write with puts or a Tcl channel.

A direct module (DIRECT-MODULES.md). write and read take any descriptor and
call std::linux::sys -- the architecture's transport layer, a direct module
itself -- with no context: they expose raw process authority, which is this
module's point, the explicit low-level layer. Code that calls them is
calling into a direct module and either mediates them through a context of
its own (std::linux::io's LinuxIO does) or is declared direct itself.

== SOURCE
stdlib/linux.bot (import std::linux). A direct module (DIRECT-MODULES.md):
it exercises raw authority.

