== NOTES
The argument is an anonymous struct whose fields are named after the x86-64
Linux syscall registers and hold std::abi::x86_64::Register64 values: rax
(the syscall number, required) and rdi, rsi, rdx, r10, r8, r9 (arguments
1-6; an omitted one is zero). Another field is UNKNOWN-FIELD, a missing rax
MISSING-FIELD, and a field of another type (an AArch64 register included)
the TYPE error. It executes `syscall` and returns rax as a Register64: the
kernel's raw result, with no errno decoding, no -1 convention, no retry.
Read it with std::abi::x86_64::to_int (a negative value is -errno).

The transport knows the register convention and nothing about any
syscall: numbers and meanings are source data (stdlib/linux/sys.x86_64.bot).
It is never context-free, folded, merged, reordered or removed.

Native backends only (cranelift, cranelift-generic, executables), and only
on an x86-64 host (elsewhere NATIVE UNSUPPORTED); interp and compile refuse
the call when it runs with NATIVE-ONLY. The namespace is direct: the calling
module must be a `direct module` or mediate the call through a context of
its own. Ordinary programs use std::linux::write, std::linux::read,
std::linux::io or io instead.

