@topic stdlib.module.std::linux::fs
title: std::linux::fs -- standard namespace
summary: Linux's native paths and its synchronous filesystem, through the raw syscalls of stdlib/linux.bot (WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-7.md).
index: stdlib
symbol: std::linux::fs

== DESCRIPTION
  with context std::linux::fs::create()               install it (entry program)

  std::linux::fs::open(path, mode)          an owned File (std::io::fs::OpenMode)
  std::linux::fs::read(file, count)         one read(2): a Read {file, data}
  std::linux::fs::read_all(file)            reads to the end of the file
  std::linux::fs::write(file, data)         one write(2): a Written {file, count}
  std::linux::fs::write_all(file, data)     writes all of DATA: the File
  std::linux::fs::close(file)               releases the File
  std::linux::fs::read_file(path)           every byte of a file
  std::linux::fs::write_file(path, data)    create or replace
  std::linux::fs::create_file(path, data)   create a file that did not exist
  std::linux::fs::entries(dir)              every entry, its native name
  std::linux::fs::create_directory(path), remove_file(path),
  remove_directory(path)

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Native paths.

A Linux name is bytes: any byte but 0 (and "/", which separates components).
NativePath holds exactly the bytes the kernel takes or returns -- non-empty,
without 0, nothing else assumed: they need not be UTF-8, and nothing is
normalized -- so every name a directory holds round-trips: listed, joined to
its directory and opened again, it names the same entry
(audit/filesystem/experiments/linux.tcl: a lossy textual name of the bytes
FF FE 78 opened another file). A textual std::linux::path::LinuxPath has
exactly one NativePath, its UTF-8 bytes (std::linux::path::native); a
NativePath has a String only when its bytes are UTF-8 (`text`, else
std::io::path::NotText), and `display` is the explicitly lossy one for
people, never an identity. Two NativePaths are equal exactly when their
bytes are: path equality is spelling, never file identity ("a", "./a" and a
link to it are three paths).

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Authority and ownership.

LinuxFS is the filesystem context: the authority to open names, create and
remove entries and list directories, relative to the process's working
directory (AT_FDCWD). `linux` is a direct module, so every function here
that makes a syscall requires LinuxFS (DIRECT-MODULES.md): the raw authority
reaches callers only as that context, and nothing above std::linux::fs is
direct for using it. A path is a request, never authority.

A File is an opened file: an opaque token of its descriptor, AFFINE -- its
one owner may use it, give it away (move it into a call, a result, a struct
field) and close it; it cannot be copied (USE-AFTER-MOVE), and a closed File
no longer exists. Its affinity comes from its one other field, a
std::io::fs::Owner, an affine token that owns nothing and costs one word:
Botlish has no declaration that makes a struct affine (the report, "Affine
ownership"). Operations that use a File take it and give it back: `read`
returns a Read {file, data}, `write` a Written {file, count}, write_all the
File.

Ownership is not cleanup. Nothing closes a File for its owner: a File
dropped unclosed, by an owner that returns, fails or ends without closing
it, leaks its descriptor until the process ends (the language releases an
affine value without running code: AFFINE-VALUES.md). Close every File you
open; the whole-file operations below do it on every path. One rule closes
for you: a read or write that FAILS closes its File (close's own result
ignored) and fails with the operation's error, so a failed operation never
leaves an owner holding a broken File, and an error path never leaks.

Operations on a File require LinuxFS too: the descriptor is used by syscalls
of the direct module `linux`, which only a context of this module mediates.
(On Windows, where a HANDLE is used by ordinary C calls, an opened File
needs no context: std::windows::fs.)

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Completion.

`read` and `write` are one syscall each: a read may return fewer bytes than
asked (no bytes is the end of the file: a read always asks for at least
one), a write may write fewer than given. read_all, write_all and the
whole-file operations complete: they repeat until the end of the file, or
until every byte is written; a write that makes no progress is a failure
(Other), never a loop. No operation retries a failure (EINTR included: a
regular file's I/O is not interrupted by a handled signal on Linux) or
promises durability: a completed write is in the kernel, not necessarily on
the disk (no fsync).

Every failure is std::io::fs::FileError: the reason (`reason_of`) and the
kernel's errno, never a path validation error.

