== DESCRIPTION
std::linux::sys is an architecture-specific module: it has no
stdlib/linux/sys.bot but this file and stdlib/linux/sys.aarch64.bot, and the
compilation target's architecture selects the one that defines it
(surface/modules.tcl's ModuleFile). Both files declare the same members with
the same signatures (tests/linux-syscall.test pins it), so code above them
-- linux, std::linux::io, io -- is one source on every architecture. What
differs is all here, as ordinary Botlish data: which syscall number, which
register carries which argument, and the architecture's register type and
bridges.

  std::linux::sys::write(fd, data)   write(2), SYS_write = 1: rdi = fd, rsi =
                                the address of data's payload, rdx = its
                                byte count. The raw signed rax as an Int.
