== ERRORS
    LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun

A call must handle each (on NAME:), admit it in its function's errors
clause, or be proven unable to raise it (rule.unhandled-errors).

== USE
An intrinsic of namespace std::mutable_array: import std::mutable_array,
then write std::mutable_array::copy. Declared nomethod: method syntax does
not apply (NOMETHOD-CALL); call it as std::mutable_array::copy(...).

== BACKENDS
every backend (interp, compile, cranelift-generic, cranelift)

== NOTES
Memmove semantics: dst and src may be the same array with overlapping
ranges, and the result is as if every source element were read before
any write. Both ranges are half-open slices, checked like
std::str::substring's: the destination ds..ds+count against dst's capacity
first, then the source ss..ss+count; the first invalid one fails
(LowerUnderrun for a bound below its interval, a negative count
included; UpperOverrun above it) and nothing is copied. Returns unit; dst
must be a place. Declared nomethod: the first argument is the
destination, so dst.copy(...) would misread as copying dst.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::from_list([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])
std::mutable_array::copy(a, 1, a, 0, 3)
a.freeze(5)
@caption An overlapping copy within one array
@value [1, 1, 2, 3, 5]
@end

