@topic stdlib.module.std::mutable_array
title: std::mutable_array -- standard namespace
summary: MutableArray[T]: a fixed-length mutable value with indexed reads and writes (at, set, swap), slice copies and List snapshots (freeze).
index: stdlib
symbol: std::mutable_array
keywords: array; fixed size array; mutable array; buffer; table
related: concept.mutable-array concept.values stdlib.module.std::mutable_vector proof.list-bounds

== DESCRIPTION
The namespace's intrinsics are root natives registered under their qualified
names -- std::mutable_array::allocate, ::create, ::from_list, ::generate,
::capacity, ::at, ::set, ::swap, ::copy, ::freeze (core/mutarray.tcl) -- and
this module may never define a member of the same name (surface/modules.tcl,
DUPLICATE-NATIVE). The one member below is an ordinary function over those
intrinsics (STDLIB-NAMESPACES.md):

  std::mutable_array::get(array, i, d)     the element at i, or D when i does
                                      not designate an element: exactly
                                      std::mutable_array::at(array, i) with its
                                      IndexNotFound -- and nothing else --
                                      handled by returning D.

The constructors are intrinsics, each with a static ownership role for what
it does with the value it is given (core/native.tcl's -ownership):

  std::mutable_array::create(n, x)      x is *repeated*: one value placed in n
                                   slots, so x must be unrestricted (an
                                   affine x is AFFINE-DUPLICATION-
                                   UNSUPPORTED)
  std::mutable_array::from_list(xs)     xs *moves* into the array, which owns
                                   every element it had
  std::mutable_array::generate(n, f)    f is a *factory*: called once per slot,
                                   in index order, each result owned by
                                   its slot (affine results included)

They used to be ordinary functions here, typed at their calls by a
precomputed container rule (hir/containers.tcl) that hid their bodies from
the ownership discipline: `create(2, make_coroutine())` compiled and stored
one coroutine in two slots. An intrinsic's roles are what the discipline
reads, so no library body can do what the same body written in a program
could not (MUTABLE-ARRAY.md, "Library ownership").

get's DEFAULT is an ordinary argument, evaluated before the call like any
other (no lazy default), and it is not an optional/null result: a present
index gives the element, IndexNotFound gives DEFAULT, and every other
failure of std::mutable_array::at (a non-MutableArray or non-Int argument)
propagates unchanged. get copies the element out, so an array of affine
elements cannot use it (its at is AFFINE-ELEMENT-COPY-OUT): swap the element
out instead.

== SOURCE
stdlib/mutable_array.bot (import std::mutable_array).

== MEMBERS
    std::mutable_array::allocate   intrinsic -- A new MutableArray of n slo...
    std::mutable_array::at         intrinsic -- The element of a MutableArr...
    std::mutable_array::capacity   intrinsic -- The number of slots of a Mu...
    std::mutable_array::copy       intrinsic -- Copies count elements of sr...
    std::mutable_array::create     intrinsic -- A new MutableArray of n slo...
    std::mutable_array::element    intrinsic -- The place of slot i of a Mu...
    std::mutable_array::freeze     intrinsic -- An immutable List of the fi...
    std::mutable_array::from_list  intrinsic -- A new MutableArray holding ...
    std::mutable_array::generate   intrinsic -- A new MutableArray of n slo...
    std::mutable_array::get        function
    std::mutable_array::set        intrinsic -- Stores x in slot i of a Mut...
    std::mutable_array::swap       intrinsic -- Stores x in slot i of a Mut...

Each member is a topic: -help NAME (e.g. -help
std::mutable_array::allocate).

== NOTES
A MutableArray is a value, not a reference: `b = a` makes an independent
logical copy (copy-on-write underneath), so a mutation through a is
never seen through b. Its length (capacity) is fixed when it is made.
Mutations (set, swap, copy into it) change the array in place, so their
receiver must be a place -- a local, parameter or context parameter, or a
field path from one -- and a temporary receiver is MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER.

Make one with allocate (slots of unit), create (one value repeated),
from_list or generate (a factory per slot). Read with at or
std::mutable_array::get, write with set or swap, take snapshots with freeze,
and visit the elements with `loop x in a:`. There is no equality or hash
for arrays. An array of affine elements (coroutine handles, ...) is
itself affine: elements move in and out only through set, swap and
consuming loops, and at would copy one out (AFFINE-ELEMENT-COPY-OUT).

