A MutableMap is a mutable VALUE, like MutableVector: `n = m` (or passing m,
returning it, storing it in a struct) makes a logical copy, and mutating one
copy never changes another. The mutating operations take the map as a place
parameter (PLACE-PARAMETERS.md): `m.set(k, v)` updates the caller's own map
in place. A loop iterates the map as it was when the loop began: mutating
the map in the body changes the map, never what the loop visits.

Keys are compared by Botlish equality (`==`) and placed by a keyed hash the
program cannot observe: the iteration order is insertion order, whatever the
hash, on every backend and in every process. Removing a key and setting it
again adds it last. Expected cost: O(1) amortized for contains, at, set and
remove; O(1) for length; O(n) for a loop; a growth or compaction is O(n),
amortized over the operations before it.

A map's identity is its associations; its order is a permutation of its keys
(PROVEN-COLLECTION-REORDERING.md). reorder changes the order only: every key
keeps its stored key and its value. Its order must be known, at the call, to
hold exactly the map's keys (REORDER-NOT-PROVEN otherwise): from order(), or
from validate_order, which checks a candidate once. A proven reorder checks
nothing; it is O(n) and hashes nothing again.

The representation is std::ordered_table's, opaque: no program sees a slot,
a hash, a capacity or a removed entry.

