@topic stdlib.module.std::test
title: std::test -- standard namespace
summary: The golden test framework, an ordinary Botlish library: a suite context, std::test::value, std::test::holds and std::test::rejects cases, and std::test::report.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::test
keywords: testing; unit tests; test framework; assertions; golden tests
related: concept.testing concept.negative-tests

== DESCRIPTION
A golden test runs an operation, observes its outcome, compares the outcome
with an expected one, and records the comparison as data. There is no test
syntax, no assertion, no test-only native and no hidden registry: a suite is
an ordinary context value holding an ordinary MutableVector, registration is
an ordinary call that pushes a deferred check into it, and the runner is an
ordinary coroutine that runs the checks in registration order and yields
their results.

  import std::compiler
  import std::test

  fn register_arithmetic_tests():
      fn adds() errors std::compiler::CompilerError:
          static compile:
              20 + 22
      std::test::value("adds", 42, adds)
      fn unbound() errors std::compiler::CompilerError:
          static compile:
              missing + 1
      std::test::rejects("unbound", std::compiler::DiagnosticCode::Unbound, unbound)

  with context std::test::suite()
  register_arithmetic_tests()
  std::test::report()     # ["PASS adds", "PASS unbound", "2 passed, 0 failed"]

The suite. `std::test::suite()` is the value a program installs with `with
context`: a Suite whose one field is the MutableVector of registered cases.
The context value itself is an ordinary immutable struct, installed once
like any context (entry program top level only, at most one per program,
CONTEXTS.md); registration mutates the vector it holds
(`suite.cases.push(...)`), never the binding -- the place model of
MUTABLE-VECTOR.md. Calling a registration function where no suite is
installed is the ordinary MISSING-CONTEXT.

Registration. `std::test::case(name, check)` records a TestCase {name, run}
whose `run` is a function value of the one uniform type Fn{args: [], return:
Match}: registration stores it and never calls it. A function value is the
deferred representation because Botlish coroutines start eagerly --
`coroutine {step, first} = f()` runs f to its first yield at once
(COROUTINES.md 15) -- so storing a started coroutine would run the test
while registering it. The value helpers below (`std::test::value`,
`std::test::holds`, `std::test::rejects`, ...) wrap a test body into such a
check while its concrete types are known: they call the body, turn the one
error a body may complete with, std::compiler::CompilerError, into a
mismatch, compare, and return a Match. A body is a function of no arguments
whose only declared error is std::compiler::CompilerError: any other error
the body may raise must be handled inside it (the compiler rejects the
registration otherwise, at the call: the body's error set does not fit the
parameter contract `Fn{args: [], return: any, errors:
[std::compiler::CompilerError]}` the helper's code infers). Botlish has no
catch-all handler, so this is how an arbitrary test outcome becomes data
without one.

Matchers. A Match is the verdict of one comparison, as data: {matched,
expected, actual, detail}, the three texts saying what was expected, what
was observed and anything further (a diagnostic's message). A mismatch is a
value, never an error: a failing test does not stop the run.
`std::test::equal` compares with `==`; `std::test::satisfies` applies a
caller's predicate; `std::test::diagnostic` compares a rejected block's
std::compiler::DiagnosticCode (never its message); `std::test::passed()` and
`std::test::mismatch(...)` build verdicts for a custom check.

Running. `std::test::run()` is the runner coroutine: started with `coroutine
{step, first} = std::test::run()`, it runs one case per segment, in
registration order, and sends that case's result as a one-element List; its
last segment sends the empty List and completes. (A coroutine's yields and
its return share one outward type and Botlish has no sum type with a
payload, so "one more result" and "no more results" are a List of one result
and the empty List: a batch of results.) Every start runs every registered
case again: a suite may be run any number of times, each run calling each
check exactly once. `std::test::results()` drains a run,
`std::test::report()` formats it: one "PASS name" line per passing case, a
"FAIL name" line and its indented "expected:", "actual:" and "detail:" lines
per failing one, then "P passed, F failed".

Text. An Int's text is std::int::to_string's (NUMERIC-TEXT-BOUNDARY-
MILESTONE-1.md); a value's is this module's own (`describe`, slices of one
table for its punctuation), and an enum has no String form (ENUMS.md), so
`code_name` spells every std::compiler::DiagnosticCode case itself.

