@topic stdlib.module.std::web
title: std::web -- standard namespace
summary: URI percent-encoding (std::web::uri_escape_text) and the refinement types std::web::Emailish and std::web::UriQueryValue with their proving predicates.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::web
keywords: url encoding; percent encoding; uri escape; email validation; web
related: concept.refinements stdlib.module.std::str

== DESCRIPTION
uri_escape_text is the executable percent-encoding algorithm NATIVE-URI-
ESCAPE.md moved out of stdlib/web.tcl's uriEscape native -native-body (raw
core IR, pasted into every call site) and into this ordinary cross-file
Botlish module definition, so it is resolved and compiled once, like any
other function, instead of copied. It is the same algorithm as the removed
stdlib/web.tcl native's Tcl implementation: percent-encode every UTF-8 byte
of s except the RFC 3986 unreserved characters (A-Za-z0-9._~-), as uppercase
%XX. It reads s one character at a time with std::str::char_at
(core/strings.tcl: str -> UnicodeChar, no allocation) and
std::char::scalar_value, and writes a non-ASCII scalar's UTF-8 bytes from
the scalar with bit arithmetic (esc_scalar), so it builds neither a
one-character String per position nor a std::str::encode_utf8 List per
character (ENCODE-UTF8-ALLOCATION-RESEARCH.md).

high_nibble extracts the high half of a byte and normalizes it to a logical
nibble via the Byte/Nibble/HighNibble scalar domains and typed bit
arithmetic (core/scalarbits.tcl, stdlib/byte.bot; see
BYTE-NIBBLE-BIT-ARITHMETIC.md): std::byte::high_nibble(b) is the position-
preserving extraction (b & 0xF0), and std::byte::nibble(...) normalizes that
to the logical 0..15 value hex_pair/hex_digits index with.

is_unreserved (ASCII-AND-URI-UNRESERVED.md) is now the compositional form
the earlier milestones' own comments already anticipated: ASCII letters or
digits (std::ascii::is_alphanumeric, stdlib/ascii.bot -- structural fact,
owned by the "std::ascii" namespace) OR membership in
additional_unreserved_chars, the four-byte '-' '.' '_' '~' set RFC 3986
additionally exempts from percent-encoding (URI *policy*, owned here, never
folded into ascii::'s own structural classes). The set is built once, from
std::byte::set(['-', '.', '_', '~']) (stdlib/byte.bot,
BYTE-SET.md/MINIMAL-IMMUTABLE-SET.md's retained-module-value machinery), and
reused by every is_unreserved call -- never reconstructed per call. This is
now the clean, handler-free initializer STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md
restores: the call-specific completion proof (hir/completions.tcl) proves
every element of this literal list is statically in Byte's own domain, so
std::byte::set's own declared AboveRange error is proven impossible for this
exact call -- no handler, no placeholder fallback set, no `on` clause.

is_unreserved's callers pass it an ASCII scalar value as a Byte with no
conversion: std::char::scalar_value's result is nonnegative and each call is
guarded by `v <= 127` (an `and` operand or an `if` branch), so the range
analysis proves 0..127, inside Byte's domain (no std::byte::from_int, no
handler).

esc_from/esc_scalar accumulate their result by repeated concat, the same
recursive idiom examples/stdlib/string_reverse.bot's reverse_from and
examples/stdlib/ai_text_clean.bot's clean_from already use.

Emailish and UriQueryValue are this module's refinement types
(REFINEMENT-VALUES.md): ordinary Botlish declarations, `refined type NAME =
str`, each a new nominal type whose values are exactly Strings proven to
satisfy its proposition, with the exact runtime representation of str (no
wrapper, tag or allocation). This module is their owner: only its own
functions may declare a proof of them (`proves`), and each has exactly one
such function here, emailish? and uri_query_value?. Code anywhere may call
them, pass a proven value where the refinement is required, and use a
refined value wherever a str is expected; nothing outside this module can
forge the proof. The compiler knows neither name: every fact it derives
about them comes from these declarations.

== SOURCE
stdlib/web.bot (import std::web).

== MEMBERS
    std::web::Emailish             type
    std::web::UriQueryValue        type
    std::web::additional_unreserved_chars value
    std::web::emailish?            function
    std::web::hex_digits           value
    std::web::is_unreserved        function
    std::web::local_extra_chars    value -- local_extra_chars is the establ...
    std::web::uri_escape_text      function
    std::web::uri_query_value?     function -- uri_query_value?(value) deci...

Each member is a topic: -help NAME (e.g. -help std::web::Emailish).

== NOTES
Ordinary Botlish. A program that imports web also prints the module's own
ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL findings (its tables of one-character Strings),
which are listed in the warning gate's manifest; they are not about the
importing program.

