@topic stdlib.module.std::windows
title: std::windows -- standard namespace
summary: The Windows platform's low-level output operations, thin ordinary-Botlish wrappers over kernel32 (WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-2.md).
index: stdlib
symbol: std::windows

== DESCRIPTION
  std::windows::standard_handle(device)   GetStdHandle: the process's
                                          standard handle of DEVICE, as it is
                                          now
  std::windows::write_file(handle, data)  WriteFile: ONE kernel32 call, all
                                          of DATA asked (or a DWORD's worth);
                                          a WriteResult
  std::windows::last_error()              GetLastError: the calling thread's
                                          last-error code, as it is now

kernel32 is the boundary: the Windows system interface is its DLLs' exports,
which this module calls through ordinary `extern c fn` declarations of their
documented prototypes (C-FFI.md), linked through kernel32's import library
(`extern c library "kernel32"`): no system call number, no C runtime
function, and no compiler knowledge of I/O.

A HANDLE is an opaque pointer-sized value, `void *` -- a std::ffi::c::Ptr[
std::ffi::c::Void] here, never an integer or a POSIX descriptor. A standard
handle is the process's (its STD_INPUT_HANDLE, STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE and
STD_ERROR_HANDLE slots), *borrowed*: this module never closes one, and has
no CloseHandle. GetStdHandle documents two results that are no usable
handle: NULL when the process has no handle for the device (a process
started without one), and INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE when the call itself failed.
NULL is told apart (`null?`); INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE is a pointer value
Botlish cannot compare with (a C pointer has no equality, C-ABI-TYPES.md),
and is left to WriteFile, which refuses it with ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE (6),
the error a WriteResult then carries.

Windows reports a failure as its API does: the call returns FALSE and the
reason is the thread's last-error code (GetLastError), which a later call
may change. write_file reads it right after WriteFile returns, before any
other call: nothing runs between the two but the test of WriteFile's BOOL,
and the byte count's write-back value is built only after it
(C-FFI-ERROR-CAPTURE-MILESTONE-1.md); neither the allocator nor the
collector makes a Win32 call that sets the code. A result never encodes the
code in its count: it holds WriteFile's own success, the count it reported,
and, separately, the code a failed call left (`last_error`) (0 when it
succeeded: the code is never read after a success, where it means nothing).

Byte storage reaches C only through the borrowing contract
(C-POINTER-BORROWING.md): the buffer is `read noescape` (exposed as it is,
no copy) and its count derived from the ONE value -- there is no count
parameter; the DWORD WriteFile writes its count into is a 4-byte
MutableBytes, `write noescape` (a private copy C writes, read back as a
binary layout). A DWORD holds at most 4294967295, so a larger Bytes is asked
for in part: a short write by construction, which the count says.

Thin by design: ONE kernel32 call per operation, no loop, no retry, no
console mode, code page or UTF-16 conversion (the bytes are written as they
are: WriteFile writes bytes to a console, a file or a pipe alike), no
buffering, no overlapped I/O, no handle ownership. The standard streams as a
context and the portable std::io::IO implementation are std::windows::io's.

An ordinary module (DIRECT-MODULES.md): every C call here retains nothing --
every pointer parameter is noescape -- so none is a direct edge. Any code
may ask the process for its standard handles; a handle authorizes only the
writes its device takes.

