@topic stdlib.module.std::windows::fs
title: std::windows::fs -- standard namespace
summary: Windows' native paths and its synchronous filesystem, through the wide Win32 functions of kernel32 (WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-7.md).
index: stdlib
symbol: std::windows::fs

== DESCRIPTION
  with context std::windows::fs::create()          install it (entry program)

  std::windows::fs::open(path, mode)          an owned File (std::io::fs::OpenMode)
  std::windows::fs::read(file, count)         one ReadFile: a Read {file, data}
  std::windows::fs::read_all(file)            reads to the end of the file
  std::windows::fs::write(file, data)         one WriteFile: a Written {file, count}
  std::windows::fs::write_all(file, data)     writes all of DATA: the File
  std::windows::fs::close(file)               CloseHandle
  std::windows::fs::read_file(path), write_file(path, data),
  create_file(path, data), entries(dir), create_directory(path),
  remove_file(path), remove_directory(path)

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Native paths.

A Windows name is UTF-16 code units, and NTFS takes any unit but a few ASCII
ones, an unpaired surrogate included: the units CreateFileW is given are the
units FindFirstFileW returns (audit/filesystem/experiments/ windows.tcl, W1,
on windows-2025's NTFS). NativePath holds exactly those units -- non-empty,
without 0, nothing else assumed -- so every name a directory lists
round-trips. A textual std::windows::path::WindowsPath has exactly one
NativePath, its UTF-16 encoding (std::windows::path::native); a NativePath
has a String only when it holds no unpaired surrogate (`text`, else
std::io::path::NotText), and `display` is the explicitly lossy one for
people: the experiment's lossy spelling of a surrogate name opened ANOTHER
file, whose name really is U+FFFD. The ANSI code page is never used: every
call is a wide (W) function, given the units' little-endian bytes and a
two-byte NUL built for that one call. Equality is spelling, never file
identity: NTFS compares names without case ("Case.txt" and "CASE.TXT" are
one file, W4), Win32 rewrites some (below), and nothing here asks the
filesystem.

Path syntax (`kind`, `join`) is Win32's, not Unix's with backslashes:

  Relative       a\b         relative to the current directory
  DriveRelative  C:a         relative to drive C's current directory
                             ("C:x" resolved to C:\x on a runner whose
                             current directory is on D:, W5): never C:\a
  DriveAbsolute  C:\a
  RootRelative   \a          the current drive's root (D:\x there)
  Unc            \\server\share\a, //server/share/a
  Device         \\.\NAME, //./NAME: the Win32 device namespace
  Verbatim       \\?\C:\a, \\?\UNC\server\share, \??\C:\a: handed to the
                 NT namespace unchanged

A drive designator is any one unit and ":" (Win32 resolved "1:x" to "1:\x",
W5). Win32 normalizes every non-verbatim path before the filesystem sees it:
"/" is a separator, "." and ".." components are resolved lexically, a
component's trailing periods and spaces are dropped ("trail." is the file
"trail", W2), the whole path is limited to MAX_PATH (a 272-unit path failed,
ERROR_PATH_NOT_FOUND, W6) and some names are devices ("NUL" in any directory
was the NUL device on windows-2025, while "CON", "COM1", "nul.txt" and
"AUX.log" made ordinary files there, W3: this depends on the Windows
version). A verbatim path is none of that: "/" is a character NTFS refuses
(ERROR_INVALID_NAME), ".." too, a trailing period is kept ("verb." is a name
of its own, which no Win32 path reaches: W2) and its length is NTFS's (32767
units).

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Authority and ownership.

WindowsFS is the filesystem context: the authority to open names, create and
remove entries and list directories, relative to the process's current
directories. The Win32 functions are declared here privately: a Win32 call
whose pointer parameters are all noescape is no direct edge
(DIRECT-MODULES.md, "Foreign is not direct"), so the context is this
module's design, not the direct-module analysis's: every public function
that names a path requires WindowsFS, and no public function takes a raw
handle. (A module that declares kernel32's CreateFileW itself has the
authority any C declaration has: the report, "Contexts".)

A File is an opened file: an opaque struct of its HANDLE, a C pointer, and
so AFFINE -- one owner, moved by use, never copied (USE-AFTER-MOVE), gone
once closed. A HANDLE is never a C runtime file descriptor: nothing here
calls the C runtime. Operations on a File need no context: the HANDLE is the
capability, used by ordinary C calls. They take the File and give it back
(Read {file, data}, Written {file, count}, write_all's File).

Ownership is not cleanup: a File dropped unclosed leaks its handle until the
process ends (the language releases an affine value without running code).
Close every File; the whole-file operations do on every path. A read or
write that FAILS closes its File (CloseHandle's own result ignored) and
fails with its error.

Files are opened with share mode READ | WRITE | DELETE (the context's
`share`): another opener -- this program or another -- may read, write,
rename or delete the file meanwhile, as on POSIX; an opener that asked for
exclusive access first makes the open fail with Busy (ERROR_SHARING_
VIOLATION, 32: W7), and so does an open after one that asked for it.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Completion and errors.

`read` and `write` are one ReadFile / WriteFile each: short counts are
ordinary, a read of no bytes is the end of the file (and so is a pipe's
ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE). read_all, write_all and the whole-file operations
complete; a write that makes no progress is Other. No retry, no durability
claim (no FlushFileBuffers). Every failure is std::io::fs::FileError: its
reason (`reason_of`) and the thread's last-error code, read right after the
failed call before anything else is called. Win32 reports a directory opened
as a file, and a directory DeleteFileW was given, as ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED (5,
W8): when the path then names a directory (GetFileAttributesW, asked after
the code was read) the reason is IsADirectory, the code still 5.

