@topic stdlib.module.std::windows::path
title: std::windows::path -- standard namespace
summary: Textual Windows paths (CONTEXT-TRAITS.md, "Paths".
index: stdlib
symbol: std::windows::path

== DESCRIPTION
  std::windows::path::validate(value)       proves VALUE a WindowsPath, or
                                            std::io::path::NotAPath
  std::windows::path::valid?(value)         true proves VALUE a WindowsPath
  std::windows::path::concat(path, part)    PATH joined with PART by Win32's
                                            syntax: a WindowsPath
  std::windows::path::text(path)            PATH's String form (the carrier)
  std::windows::path::kind(path)            its syntactic kind
                                            (std::windows::fs::PathKind)
  std::windows::path::native(path)          its std::windows::fs::NativePath

WindowsPath is a refinement of str (REFINEMENT-VALUES.md): a value proven to
be a non-empty String without U+0000 -- text that may be handed to a wide
Win32 function once it is encoded as UTF-16 and given its terminating NUL
(std::windows::fs does that, for the one call). It is the same String, with
the String's representation. It is std::linux::path::LinuxPath's and
std::darwin::path::DarwinPath's sibling, not a shared type: a Windows path
is not a Unix path with backslashes, and nothing converts one into another.

The proposition is textual eligibility and nothing more. It does NOT claim
that the path exists; that Win32 accepts it (a name holding "<", ">", "|",
'"', "?" or "*", or a ":" past a drive, is valid text that NTFS refuses or
reads as a stream); that it is shorter than MAX_PATH; that it means what its
spelling suggests -- Win32 drops a component's trailing periods and spaces,
resolves "." and "..", reads "NUL" as a device and resolves a relative,
drive-relative or root-relative path against the process's current
directories -- or that two spellings name two files (NTFS compares names
without case). Validation observes no filesystem and normalizes nothing:
"C:a", "C:\a", "\a", "\\server\share", "\\?\C:\a\." and "a//b" are valid
exactly as written, and different.

`kind` and `concat` are std::windows::fs's syntax on the path's UTF-16 units
(one rule for text and native paths): concat inserts one "\" unless the path
ends with a separator or is a bare drive designator ("C:" and "a" are "C:a",
drive C's current directory -- never "C:\a"), refuses a component that is
not itself relative ("\a", "C:a", "\\s\x", and "1:x": Win32 reads any unit
before ":" as a drive), and refuses what would not name the component
written: under a verbatim path (\\?\, \??\) a "/" or a "." or ".." component
(Win32 resolves neither there), under any other a component ending with a
period or a space (Win32 drops them).

WindowsPath satisfies std::io::path::Path through `concat` and `text`, and
std::io::fs::FilePath through them and the file operations below, which are
std::windows::fs's on the path's native path: they declare no context and
std::windows::fs's require std::windows::fs::WindowsFS, so a program using a
WindowsPath's file operations installs a WindowsFS (or is MISSING-CONTEXT at
compile time). Like everything under std::windows, this module is the
Windows platform's: compiled for another target its import is
UNSUPPORTED-PLATFORM, so no WindowsPath ever meets a Linux filesystem.

